-- (kind <> 'bootstrap') filenames"): everything seeded here predates the
-- ledger's own existence and is therefore pre-epoch history, never a
-- candidate for a future release's REQUIRED_MIGRATIONS declaration.
--
-- TWO DATA-QUALITY GAPS FOUND AND CORRECTED WHILE BUILDING THIS SEED (both
-- flagged in the affected rows' own notes column, not hidden):
--
--   1. CHECKSUM (5 rows: apply_order 51, 52, 53, 55, 63). The S0 §K JSON
--      artifact recorded either the literal placeholder text
--      "(local draft, not yet committed)" or (row 63) an 18-character value
--      with 2 extra trailing characters -- neither satisfies this table's
--      checksum_sha256 CHECK (~ '^[0-9a-f]{16}$'), and neither was ever a
--      real checksum in the first place (a pre-existing manifest-generation
--      gap, unrelated to S4). Recomputed first-party via sha256 of the
--      exact currently-committed migration file (verified against a clean
--      working tree at backend HEAD 4dd4034) -- MORE proof than the stale
--      artifact had, not an unproven assertion. Row 63's recomputed value
--      matches the JSON's own first 16 characters exactly, confirming that
--      one was a transcription artifact, not a real discrepancy.
--
--   2. APPLIED_BY (21 rows). The manifest's own "commit" column was never
--      retroactively filled in for these rows and still holds
--      authoring-time placeholder text ('pending', 'applied', '(local, not
--      yet committed)', etc). Seeding literal "pending" as applied_by into
--      a ledger whose own GOAL is "semantically truthful from day one"
--      would defeat the point of building it. Resolved first-party via
--      `git log --diff-filter=A --format=%h -- migrations/<file>` against
--      the actual committed backend history -- the same class of evidence
--      already used throughout S1-S3 of this remediation.
--
-- Neither correction touches any migration FILE's content (the "no
-- historical migration rewrites" prohibition is about file contents, not
-- this table's own metadata) and neither reopens S0-S3 architecture or
-- reclassifies any row's verification_status -- both were already fixed
-- points from the frozen S0 §K evidence; only the checksum/applied_by
-- VALUES needed real data where the source artifacts had none.
--
-- IDEMPOTENCY: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (filename) DO NOTHING -- a re-run
-- inserts zero rows.
--
-- ABSOLUTE OWNERSHIP BOUNDARY: this migration only ever SELECTs from
-- supabase_migrations.schema_migrations (read-only evidence, already
-- consumed at authoring time to produce the S0 §K classification baked
-- into the VALUES below) and only ever INSERTs into
-- public.ladieci_schema_migrations. It never writes to any Supabase-owned
-- migration-history table.

-- ── Predecessor guard ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DO $$
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
     WHERE n.nspname = 'public' AND c.relname = 'ladieci_schema_migrations'
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'S4 bootstrap-seed refused: public.ladieci_schema_migrations does not exist -- apply 2026-08-15_s4_ladieci_schema_migrations_ledger.sql first';
  END IF;
END $$;

